node_modules/
dist/
coverage/
cdk.out/
*.tsbuildinfo
*.tgz
.DS_Store
var/
# Next build artifacts of in-place template runs. The templates' own gitignore
# files are stored un-dotted (npm strips the dotted form), so they do NOT act
# as git ignores here — the root file is the only live ignore for the subtree.
.next/
next-env.d.ts
templates/skeleton/*/apps/web/out/
.claude/worktrees/
# per-checkout queue state: the tier the last close recorded. The queue CONFIG
# is composed and tracked; this is runtime state and must never be committed.
.claude/queue.state.json
# per-checkout gate rounds, one count per branch. Its own file so the counter
# cannot damage the tier above — a review round measured that cost.
.claude/gate-rounds.json
# per-run machine trace (.claude/scripts/run-journal.mjs): forensics that churn
# on every gate and collide across concurrent sessions. Unlike a committed
# evidence directory, this one is per-run and stays out of the repository.
.claude/runs/

# secrets — never commit these. The generated projects already had this block
# (templates/skeleton/*/gitignore); this repository did not, and it runs the
# loop against a Jira adapter that reads JIRA_API_TOKEN from the environment.
#
# 🔴 This block exists in THREE files, which is the shape `invariants.md` warns
# about — gitignore has no import, so the duplication is forced rather than
# chosen, and the copy nobody looks at is the one that will be wrong. What ties
# them together is a test: `dogfood.test.ts` extracts the patterns from all
# three and fails when they diverge, comparing them in ORDER.
#
# Order is load-bearing in TWO places, and neither is obvious from reading:
#   - `!.env.example` must stay BELOW `.env.*`, or the negation stops
#     re-including it;
#   - `*.env` must stay ABOVE `.env`. Git's `*` matches a leading dot, so both
#     patterns match `.env`, and `check-ignore -v` reports the LAST one — below,
#     `.env` starts reporting `*.env` as its rule. A test pins that, but it
#     fails as "wrong rule name" in a file the editor was not editing.
#
# Per the note at the top of this file, these patterns reach
# `templates/skeleton/**` too — a project that ever needs to track a file named
# `.npmrc` (registry, `node-linker`, `engine-strict`) or a fixture named `*.key`
# would find it untrackable with no error.
*.env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.envrc
.npmrc
.netrc
.pgpass
id_rsa
id_ed25519
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
*.jks
*.secret
*.secrets
*.token
*.tokens
*.password
*.passwords
*.credential
*.credentials
*.apikey
*.apikeys
*.passwd
*.creds
*.jwt
*.bearer
